# Product Requirements Document — AI Arena

## 1. Vision
Create an interactive platform where users compare AI-generated answers without seeing model identities, record preferences, and progressively receive personalized AI model recommendations.

## 2. Principles
1. Blind before reveal.
2. Randomize A/B assignment server-side.
3. Same prompt/context for both models.
4. No model identity leakage before voting.
5. Separate personal preference from global statistics.
6. Treat small samples cautiously.
7. Motion communicates state and feedback.
8. Exploration prevents early preferences from trapping the recommender.

## 3. Objectives
- Reduce model-name/brand influence.
- Collect structured human preference data.
- Learn task-specific preferences.
- Generate adaptive comparisons.
- Provide transparent analytics.
- Demonstrate full-stack AI and recommender-system engineering.

## 4. Non-Goals
The MVP will not declare a universally best AI, claim complete bias elimination, replace standardized benchmarks, expose private prompts publicly, or treat tiny samples as conclusive.

## 5. Target Users
Students, developers, researchers, AI enthusiasts, and users who regularly use multiple AI systems.

## 6. User Journey
Landing → Start → Prompt → Category → Generate → Blind Compare → Vote → Reveal → Feedback → Profile Update → Adaptive Next Comparison.

## 7. Functional Requirements
- Prompt entry and validation
- Pair selection of two distinct models
- Server-side A/B randomization
- Parallel response generation
- Blind response display
- A/B/Tie/Neither voting
- Post-vote identity reveal
- Optional feedback
- Persistent comparison/history
- Personal analytics
- Adaptive recommendations
- Exploration of under-tested models/categories

## 8. Interactive UX Requirements
### Landing
Entry animations, scroll reveals, CTA hover/press, subtle parallax, animated counters.

### Prompt
Focus transition, suggestion chips, live validation, category selection, loading state.

### Generation
Skeletons, progressive rendering where supported, neutral loading language, partial-generation state.

### Comparison
Card hover, copy interaction, expand/collapse, equal visual treatment.

### Voting
Selection emphasis, non-selected de-emphasis, confirmation, transition to reveal.

### Reveal
A/B identity transformation, model information entrance, feedback-chip appearance.

### Insights
Animated counters/charts, filtering, model highlighting, timeframe transitions.

### Arena
Interactive aggregate statistics and smooth filter transitions.

### History
Expandable cards and detail transitions.

### Recommendation
Animated matchup selection without leaking identities.

## 9. Motion System
Motion roles: **feedback, orientation, state, hierarchy**.

Suggested starting timings:
- Micro: 120–180 ms
- Standard: 180–300 ms
- Content: 250–500 ms
- Signature reveal: 350–700 ms

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. Avoid constant or distracting animation.

## 10. Recommendation
MVP: exposure, selection, preference rate, evidence adjustment, exploration.

Advanced: Beta-Bernoulli, Thompson Sampling, Bradley-Terry, contextual bandits.

Keep ties separate from wins/losses.

## 11. Categories
Programming, Writing, Education, Mathematics, Research, Creative, Data Analysis, Reasoning, General, Professional.

## 12. Feedback
Accuracy, Clarity, Reasoning, Conciseness, Formatting, Creativity, Code quality, Fewer errors, Depth, Other.

## 13. Analytics
Personal: comparisons, models tested, preference distribution, category preference, pairwise history, feedback, evidence/sample size.

Global: anonymized aggregate exposure and selection statistics.

## 14. Privacy
Keep provider keys server-side. Do not expose private prompts in public analytics. Support history deletion and anonymized aggregate participation.

## 15. Edge Cases
Provider failure, both providers failing, duplicate model pair, abandoned comparison, ties, long prompts, rate limits, slow providers, moderation/safety rejection.

## 16. Acceptance Criteria
A valid comparison requires two distinct models, equivalent prompt, randomized A/B, no pre-vote identity exposure, successful responses, user outcome, persistence, reveal, and updated statistics.

## 17. MVP Definition of Done
A user can compare two blind responses, vote, see the reveal, optionally give feedback, view history and preferences, and receive adaptive future matchups.
